## 12. Tactical strategy revamp — first implementation (2026-11-14)

- Replaced the former Noob Black-inspired deepest-player/ball-dependency role calculation with Volt's dynamic first-man / last-man / support assignment.
- First man is selected from predicted reachable ball slices using travel distance, turn delay, speed, boost and vertical reach. A short hysteresis window prevents role swapping when ETAs are nearly equal.
- Last man is selected by estimated ability to cover the projected goal-mouth lane, not simply by deepest position. A separate support role is assigned in 3v3.
- Retired the scripted 3v3 transition bump and guessed-contact receive sequence. The third player now continuously occupies a separated support lane and can respond to the actual next play.
- Added a new `tactical_rotation` routine: 1v1 uses a moving shadow/goal-mouth target; 2v2 uses first-man pressure plus a goal-side anchor; 3v3 adds a separated support lane. It uses turn-aware speed and conservative boost so defensive players do not abandon their lane for boost.
- Existing shot finding and shot-execution routines remain in place. This is a first strategic layer replacement, not a validated final balance pass.
- No compile, simulation, or in-game tests have been run yet. Review the role assignments and defensive positioning in live matches before tuning the distances/weights.


## 13. Simple challenger / shadow / boost rotation (2026-11-14)

- Replaced the complicated ball-dependency role election with a simple three-role rotation.
- Challenger is selected by a realistic approach score using distance, horizontal facing/turn cost, speed and boost reserve; a short hysteresis margin prevents near-equal candidates swapping every packet.
- Shadow is selected from the remaining players by goal-side position, centrality and ability to face the play. It uses the existing defensive positioning routine.
- In 3v3, the third player is kept out of the ball challenge and sent to a safe active boost pad while the challenger and shadow contest. If no safe pad is available, it holds a separated support lane.
- In 2v2, one player challenges and the other shadows. In 1v1, the sole player remains the challenger.
- Removed the old scripted transition bump/receive sequence from the active run path.
- Teammates outside the challenger role are blocked from normal shot selection; emergency defence remains higher priority.
- This is a structural first pass. It has not been compiled or tested in-game.


## 14. Immediate first-man challenge handoff (2026-11-14)

- On the reverted live branch, first man was being held by role election even after its own intercept became unwinnable. Teammates could remain in cover/transition positions while an opponent took the ball.
- Added a live lost-challenge check comparing the active routine's remaining intercept estimate, the car's ETA, teammate ETAs and opponent ETAs.
- When the elected challenger is beaten to the ball, or a teammate has a clearly earlier viable route, it broadcasts `CHALLENGE_RELEASED` immediately.
- Teammates exclude that released player from first-man selection for a short handoff window, while still considering the player for the normal deepest/cover/transition rotation. The next eligible teammate becomes challenger.
- The former challenger cancels its stale shot and returns to the existing defensive/rotation routine. A short takeover grace period prevents every teammate from immediately releasing in sequence against the same opponent.
- Emergency defence remains higher priority and is not released by this logic.
- This is a timing/ETA handoff, not a guarantee of a successful touch. No compile or in-game test has been run.


## 15. Risk-first challenger commitment (2026-11-14)

- The elected first man must not stop pressuring simply because the prediction system cannot find a likely winning touch. If no reliable shot/intercept is available and the stack is clear, it uses the existing `short_shot` routine as a direct, low-confidence challenge toward the opponent goal.
- While that routine is active, the challenger broadcasts `RISK_CHALLENGE` with a low score and a rough contact-time estimate. This means "I am committing to pressure, but do not assume I will win or touch the ball."
- The designated goalie remains the defensive fallback. In 3v3, the transition player treats `RISK_CHALLENGE` as an active commitment and prepares for the loose-ball follow-up; it does not start the transition bump play for a risk challenge.
- `RISK_CHALLENGE` is an accepted teamplay status and is included in the message's `shot_type` field. Teammates recognize it as an active commitment rather than interpreting it as `NO_SHOT`.
- Emergency defence remains higher priority and is not replaced by this risk-first behaviour.
- This uses an existing routine and message protocol; it has not been compiled or tested in-game.
